2 CONT Barnes, Thomas C., Thomas H. Naylor, and Charles W. Polzer.  '''''Northern New Spain, A Research Guide''''', page 102.  ...  From December 1739 to August 1744, he served as Governor of Coahuila, New Spain (Mexico).
2 CONT
2 CONT McEnroe, Sean F.  '''''From Colony to Nationhood in Mexico: Laying the Foundations, 1560-1840''''', Cambridge University Press, page 47  ...  http://books.google.com/books?id=aNFdSTGZBpQC&pg=PA48&lpg=PA48&dq=Garc%C3%ADa+de+Pruneda
2 CONC +family+in+spain&source=bl&ots=9jXdheymwr&sig=hG6xkBO39oFGs-ycdOgsGkAQxdo&hl=en&sa=X&ei=DCWyUd-tDpS8qAGfoIG4BQ&ved=0CEQQ6AEwAzgK#v=onepage&q=Garc%C3%ADa%20de%20Pruneda%20family%20in%20spain&f=false  ...  "In 1709, '''Juan García d
2 CONC e Pruneda''', a landowner in the Valley of Pilón, found himself in a fortunate position.  His father, Luis García de Pruneda (governor of the New Kingdom of León), and his mother Juana de León (descendant of Alonso de León) along 
2 CONC with his uncle, Cipriano García de Pruneda, opened a path for his ambitions.  When Juan García de Pruneda petitioned for the recognition of his privileges in the Valley of Pilón, he used much the same language and logic that was r
2 CONC outinely employed in the previous century.  Though the term ''encomienda'' was by then under a cloud of suspicion, Juan García invoked all other forms of authority commonly recognized in the valley.  He called himself a vecino and
2 CONC  cattle rancher of the Valley of Pilón, a descendant of ''conquistadores'' (including Alonso de León), and a faithful vassal of the king.  He noted that his family had always ruled over the descendants of two Chichimec leaders and
2 CONC  their followers:  Aseama, captain of the Piehuanes and Zuñigas, and Ariscapanes, captain of the Borrados and Pelones.  García made his claim to the protectorate based on a family ''encomienda'', but he did so without requesting t
2 CONC he now legally suspect title ''encomendero''.  Juan García asked to be confirmed as ''protector y amparador'' of these populations and promised, in return, to pay the Indians for their labor and to ensure their religious instructi
2 CONC on through the ''doctrina'' of Cadereyta."
2 CONT
2 CONT Villanueva de Cavazos, Lilia E. ''' ''Testamentos Coloniales de Monterrey''''', Universidad Atonoma de Nuevo Leon, Monterrey, N.L., Mexico, 1991, page 145-153.  ...  The '''1753 will of General Juan Garcia de Pruneda''' is printed
2 CONC  here and accompanied by the photographed last page of the original will.  [Protocolos, Tomo 15, Folio 266, No. 125].  The names of his deceased parents, his deceased wife, his wife's deceased parents, and his living children as w
2 CONC ell as the number of his deceased children and his place of burial are cited among his bequests. 
2 CONT --------------------
